{"type":"message","id":"ee2130e7","parentId":"8c58b971","timestamp":"2026-04-15T09:12:01.419Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the task data. I need to:\n\n1. Check task board latest task status\n2. Update my last heartbeat time\n3. If any task hasn't been updated in 60+ minutes, mark as Dead\n4. Summarize progress to shared pool work progress library\n\nCurrent time: 2026-04-15 09:11 UTC\n\nLet me look at the tasks and their updated_at times to find any that haven't been updated in 60+ minutes.\n\nLooking at the tasks:\n- Most tasks have `updated_at` as millisecond timestamps\n- Current time in ms: 2026-04-15 09:11 UTC = approximately 1775206260000 ms (let me calculate)\n\nActually, let me use the task data. The most recently updated tasks seem to be:\n- de0e7a44-6ad3-4aec-a55c-cf90202a3301 (每日销售指标达成日报) - updated_at: 1748507132766\n- d0eed428-af90-4263-af7f-0f899bdd2131 (工作日报提交表单) - updated_at: 1748507137940\n- bb0c302e-863e-4596-8043-96a132ac9adb (销售外拓计划) - updated_at: 1748507142888\n- 6c749284-e3f7-4c91-b5f3-64971c13bb5d (每日外拓记录反馈) - updated_at: 1748507147810\n\nLet me check: 1748507147810 ms → Convert to date\n1748507147810 / 1000 = 1748507147.81 seconds\nThat's around May 2025... Wait that doesn't seem right.\n\n\nActually, the current time is April 2026. So these timestamps are actually in the past. I need to figure out what the actual current timestamp is and then calculate the difference from when each task was last updated to determine if any have been dormant for 60+ minutes.\n\nLooking at the updated_at timestamps, most appear to be from 2025 or earlier, which suggests they're historical records. I should convert these timestamps to actual dates and identify which ones haven't been modified recently to flag as potentially dead tasks.\n\nThe cron expression \"0 9 * * *\" indicates this runs at 9:00 AM daily, so the current time of 9:11 AM means it's shortly after execution. For the 60-minute threshold, I need to check if any task's updated_at timestamp is older than 60 minutes from now. Most of the tasks I'm seeing have updated_at values from 2025, which would definitely exceed this threshold. However, many of these older tasks appear to be recurring or template-based items that shouldn't necessarily be marked as dead. I should look for tasks with more recent update times to determine which ones are actually stale versus which are just historical records. I'm converting the updated_at timestamp to a readable format—1775867413807 milliseconds divided by 1000 gives 1775867413 seconds, which based on the epoch conversion would be around 2026. But that doesn't align with the context suggesting May 2025, so I should verify this calculation more carefully against the known reference point of 1748507147810 milliseconds. The timestamp 1775867413807 converts to approximately March 2026, which aligns with being roughly 2 months before the current date in mid-April 2026. The key distinction is between `created_at` (when the task was first created) and `updated_at` (the most recent modification), and for staleness checking, I should use `updated_at` since that reflects when the task was last worked on.\n\nGiven the ~60 minute threshold for this check, tasks with `updated_at` values around 1775867413 seconds would be about 17 days old, making them potentially stale rather than actively being worked on. However, many of these tasks appear to be recurring or repeating tasks (some with `repeat_rule` fields set), so their age doesn't necessarily indicate they're abandoned. The real issue is that the task system only provides `updated_at` timestamps without offering a direct way to mark tasks as \"Dead\" through the available API actions. I should search for the shared pool work progress library mentioned in the task description to understand where this summary should be reported, rather than trying to force a status update through the limited task management API."},{"type":"text","text":"Let me check the shared pool bitable for the work progress library location and update my heartbeat.\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_tta39ag2811h_1","name":"feishu_bitable_app","arguments":{"action":"list"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_tta39ag2811h_2","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/gem/workspace/agent/workspace/memory/heartbeat-state.json"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"minimax-token-plan","model":"MiniMax-M2.7-highspeed","usage":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":0,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1776244288815,"responseId":"062e894080fcb61c22c7fd5ecd3cbff9"}}
